{"dir":"in","msg":{"method":"item/agentMessage/delta","params":{"threadId":"thr_1","turnId":"turn_1","itemId":"msg_1","delta":", Mara climbed the lighthouse stairs before the fog arrived.\n\nThere were 137 steps, worn smooth by generations of keepers. At the top waited the lantern room, its windows shining over the black water, and beyond them, the sea—vast, restless, and full of things Mara had never seen.\n\nHer grandfather had taught her the light’s rhythm: sweep, pause, sweep, pause. Ships trusted it. Fishermen steered by it. Even the gulls seemed to circle in its golden breath.\n\n“Light is a promise,” he used to say. “It tells the lost that someone is watching.”\n\nAfter he died, Mara inherited the lighthouse and the lonely island with it. She kept the lamp polished, the machinery oiled, and the logbook filled. Yet each night, when the beam crossed the waves, she wondered whether anyone was truly watching for her.\n\nOne November evening, a storm rose without warning. Wind struck the tower like a fist. Rain rattled the windows, and the sea vanished beneath sheets of darkness. Mara hurried to the lantern room and found the lamp stuttering.\n\n“No,” she whispered.\n\nThe generator had flooded. She seized the emergency oil can and climbed onto the narrow platform outside. Lightning flashed, revealing waves towering higher than the island cliffs. With one hand gripping the rail, she reached for the jammed fuel valve.\n\nA wave exploded against the rocks below. The tower shuddered.\n\nThen, through the storm, Mara heard a sound: three distant blasts.\n\nA ship.\n\nShe pulled the valve with all her strength. It opened. Oil rushed into the burner, and the lamp flared alive.\n\nThe beam swept across the sea.\n\nOnce. Twice.\n\nOn the third pass, it caught the ship—a small cargo vessel pitching wildly beyond the reef. Its horn answered. Mara watched as it turned, inch by inch, following the light toward safer water.\n\nShe stayed beside the lamp until dawn.\n\nAt sunrise, the storm had passed. The ship lay anchored in the bay, its crew waving from the deck. One sailor rowed ashore and climbed the path carrying a wooden box.\n\nInside was a brass compass, tarnished but beautiful.\n\n“Found it in the captain’s cabin,” he said. “It belonged to your grandfather. He gave it to us years ago, when our engine failed.”\n\nMara held the compass in her palm. Its needle trembled, then pointed steadily toward the lighthouse.\n\nFor the first time since she had become keeper, the island did not feel empty.\n\nThat night, as the beam began its patient sweep, Mara smiled.\n\nSomeone was watching after all."},"emittedAtMs":1793784772613}}
{"dir":"in","msg":{"method":"item/completed","params":{"item":{"type":"agentMessage","id":"msg_1","text":"Every evening, Mara climbed the lighthouse stairs before the fog arrived.\n\nThere were 137 steps, worn smooth by generations of keepers. At the top waited the lantern room, its windows shining over the black water, and beyond them, the sea—vast, restless, and full of things Mara had never seen.\n\nHer grandfather had taught her the light’s rhythm: sweep, pause, sweep, pause. Ships trusted it. Fishermen steered by it. Even the gulls seemed to circle in its golden breath.\n\n“Light is a promise,” he used to say. “It tells the lost that someone is watching.”\n\nAfter he died, Mara inherited the lighthouse and the lonely island with it. She kept the lamp polished, the machinery oiled, and the logbook filled. Yet each night, when the beam crossed the waves, she wondered whether anyone was truly watching for her.\n\nOne November evening, a storm rose without warning. Wind struck the tower like a fist. Rain rattled the windows, and the sea vanished beneath sheets of darkness. Mara hurried to the lantern room and found the lamp stuttering.\n\n“No,” she whispered.\n\nThe generator had flooded. She seized the emergency oil can and climbed onto the narrow platform outside. Lightning flashed, revealing waves towering higher than the island cliffs. With one hand gripping the rail, she reached for the jammed fuel valve.\n\nA wave exploded against the rocks below. The tower shuddered.\n\nThen, through the storm, Mara heard a sound: three distant blasts.\n\nA ship.\n\nShe pulled the valve with all her strength. It opened. Oil rushed into the burner, and the lamp flared alive.\n\nThe beam swept across the sea.\n\nOnce. Twice.\n\nOn the third pass, it caught the ship—a small cargo vessel pitching wildly beyond the reef. Its horn answered. Mara watched as it turned, inch by inch, following the light toward safer water.\n\nShe stayed beside the lamp until dawn.\n\nAt sunrise, the storm had passed. The ship lay anchored in the bay, its crew waving from the deck. One sailor rowed ashore and climbed the path carrying a wooden box.\n\nInside was a brass compass, tarnished but beautiful.\n\n“Found it in the captain’s cabin,” he said. “It belonged to your grandfather. He gave it to us years ago, when our engine failed.”\n\nMara held the compass in her palm. Its needle trembled, then pointed steadily toward the lighthouse.\n\nFor the first time since she had become keeper, the island did not feel empty.\n\nThat night, as the beam began its patient sweep, Mara smiled.\n\nSomeone was watching after all.","phase":"final_answer","memoryCitation":null,"delivery":null,"questions":null,"startedAtMs":1793784772490,"completedAtMs":1793784782091},"threadId":"thr_1","turnId":"turn_1","completedAtMs":1793784782091},"emittedAtMs":1793784782096}}
